      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Spawning of anadromous salmon requires freshwater gravel substrates; they do not spawn in marine water.",
      "evidence": "Empirical observations in multiple salmonid species show successful fertilisation only in freshwater; marine water is physiologically unsuitable for egg development.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The Caspian Sea is a landlocked, endorheic basin with no connection to the Atlantic or Pacific oceans.",
      "evidence": "Geographic definitions (e.g., Encyclopaedia Britannica, UN geoscheme) identify the Caspian as the world’s largest enclosed inland body of water, isolated from oceanic waters by land.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "No species of the genera Salmo (salmon) or Oncorhynchus are native or established in the Caspian basin.",
      "evidence": "FAO 2022 report on Caspian fisheries and IUCN Red List distribution maps list only trout, sturgeon, and other salmonids; Salmo salar and all Oncorhynchus species are absent.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "The fish commonly called “Caspian salmon” is the brown‑trout subspecies Salmo trutta caspius, not a true salmon.",
      "evidence": "Taxonomic literature (e.g., Kottelat & Freyhof 2007) classifies Salmo trutta caspius within the brown‑trout complex, distinct from Salmo salar and Oncorhynchus spp.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Salmo trutta caspius is semi‑anadromous and spawns in freshwater tributaries of the Caspian Sea, not in the sea itself.",
      "evidence": "Regional ecological studies (e.g., Kharin 2015) document migrations of this subspecies from the Caspian into rivers such as the Volga, Kura, and Ural where spawning occurs on river gravel.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [5]
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "Therefore, salmon do not mate (spawn) in the Caspian Sea.",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 1‑6: true salmon require freshwater and are absent from the Caspian; the only fish called “Caspian salmon” also spawns in freshwater.",
